# The pre-commit floor. Fast, and honest about what is red by design.
#
# `set -e` is load-bearing. Husky runs this file with `sh`, and without it every
# command runs and only the LAST exit code decides: a Biome failure followed by a
# passing file-length scan commits anyway. A hook that reports success on a lint
# error is worse than no hook, because it is trusted.
set -e

# Lint, format and import order, on the staged files only. This is `check`, not
# `lint`: `check` is what `pnpm lint` runs, and a file that merely needs
# formatting fails it with no rule name in the output - the most confusing
# possible way to block a commit, so the hook runs the same thing the repo does.
#
# Warnings are allowed on purpose. The tree carries 10 of them (cognitive
# complexity in core's cron/diff/health/signals, `!important` in the reduced-motion
# block, one descending-specificity selector). Promoting them to errors blocks any
# commit that touches those files and teaches everyone to type `--no-verify`,
# which protects nothing.
pnpm exec biome check --staged

# Rule 1: no file over 300 lines. Whole tree, not staged files - a commit is a
# whole tree, and the rule is per-file, so a partial set can hide an overage.
node scripts/check-file-length.mjs

# Credentials, in every staged file (git ls-files reads the index, so staged work
# is covered). 0.13s, so it runs before the slow gates. This repo is public and
# needs no committed credential at all - the browser profile and the SQLite file
# are both gitignored - so a credential in a tracked file is a design violation,
# and a public commit is public forever (docs/decisions-tooling.md D-023).
node scripts/check-secrets.mjs

# The JSON Schema in schemas/ must match the zod schemas that generate it. Cheap,
# and drift here is silent everywhere else.
node scripts/generate-schemas.mjs check

# Typecheck, whole workspace: measured 1.9s (TypeScript 7 is the native compiler,
# D-018). A commit that does not compile is not a behaviour change, it is a
# broken tree, and 2 seconds is not a reason to let one exist.
pnpm typecheck

# The suite: measured 6.0s for 78 files. Rule 12 makes a test part of every
# behaviour, so the suite is the behaviour - committing a red one is how a red
# main becomes normal. The design suite (real Edge) is NOT here: see the last
# comment. Coverage is not here either, because a coverage run is minutes and a
# hook must not be.
pnpm test

# Deliberately NOT `pnpm check`: that chain ends in `pnpm test:design`, and the
# real-Edge design suite under apps/web/test/design is not written yet, so
# `check` is red at HEAD. A hook that is always red is a hook that gets bypassed.
# `check` stays the gate before declaring work done (AGENTS.md); this is the
# few-second floor that has to be green for a commit to exist at all.
